# The local APIC timer runs periodically at 100 Hz (docs/design.md, "The
# timer"). With interrupts still off, the preemption point's IRR poll sees
# the timer pending; then the kernel idles. The first tick, which may have
# waited in the IRR, is skipped; the next ten are timed against the
# calibration reference (the HPET on QEMU) and must take 100 ms within 1%.
# Each tick is dated by the timer's reload, the reference reading less the
# countdown's progress since, so the host's delay in delivering the
# interrupt (often hundreds of microseconds under TCG) does not count; the
# handler-to-handler time is printed too.
cmdline: test=ticks
exit: success
expect: microL4 kernel
expect: timer: calibrating against the HPET at 100000000 Hz
expect: timer: periodic at 100 Hz: divide by 1, vector 0xfd
expect: ticks: with interrupts off the IRR poll sees an interrupt pending; the timer's: yes
expect: ticks: idling; timing 10 ticks
expect: ticks: 10 ticks: the timer reloaded over
expect: ticks: within 100000 us within 1%
expect: TEST PASS
